From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 17:00:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] PM / devfreq: qcom-memlat: Do not poll the remote frequency
 by default

The memlat devfreq devices use the remote governor: the CPU control
processor (CPUCP) runs the memory latency algorithm and votes the DDR,
LLCC and DDR_QOS levels by itself, the kernel only reads the vote back.
The driver still sets polling_ms to cpucp_sample_ms / 2, so the devfreq
core queues the monitor every 2 ms on Kaanapali, and every poll is one
SCMI get_param round trip per monitor, only to keep trans_stat current.

On a Kaanapali (SM8850) tablet with the screen off and the system idle
this costs about 3300 CPUCP mailbox interrupts per second on CPU0, about
3200 function call IPIs per second on the other CPUs of the cluster and
the matching workqueue wakeups. CPU0 stays in WFI and the cluster and
system power domains rarely collapse. With polling_interval set to 0 the
interrupt rate drops from 7592 to 183 per second and the IPI rate from
2539 to 46 per second, with no change in the votes, which CPUCP keeps
making.

cur_freq does not depend on the polling: cur_freq_show() calls
get_cur_freq(), which asks CPUCP for the current level on every read.
Only trans_stat and the devfreq_frequency trace event need the polling.
Leave polling_ms at 0 by default; anyone who wants the transition
statistics can still write a period to polling_interval in sysfs.

Signed-off-by: mzak <mzak.amin@gmail.com>
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c | 15 ++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c b/drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c
index 9ca85b2f4c1a..6d19fc36528e 100644
--- a/drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c
+++ b/drivers/devfreq/scmi-qcom-memlat-devfreq.c
@@ -1176,12 +1176,17 @@ static int scmi_qcom_memlat_configure_events(struct scmi_device *sdev,
 		struct devfreq_dev_profile *profile = &memory->profile;
 
 		/*
-		 * CPUCP re-evaluates and re-votes at most once per cpucp_sample_ms,
-		 * so a new operating point can appear only that often. Polling at half
-		 * that period guarantees each distinct vote is observed in trans_stat
-		 * before it can change again.
+		 * CPUCP selects and votes the memory frequency on its own; the
+		 * kernel only reads the vote back. cur_freq queries CPUCP on demand
+		 * through get_cur_freq(), so periodic polling is not needed for it.
+		 * Polling only keeps trans_stat and the devfreq_frequency trace
+		 * event current, at the cost of one SCMI round trip per monitor
+		 * (and its mailbox interrupt) every poll, which keeps the CPUs out
+		 * of their deep idle states. Leave it off by default; userspace can
+		 * enable it through the polling_interval attribute. A period of half
+		 * of cpucp_sample_ms observes every distinct vote.
 		 */
-		profile->polling_ms = max(1U, info->cfg_data->cpucp_sample_ms / 2);
+		profile->polling_ms = 0;
 		profile->get_cur_freq = scmi_qcom_devfreq_get_cur_freq;
 		profile->initial_freq = range->max_freq_khz > 1 ?
 					(unsigned long)((u64)range->min_freq_khz * HZ_PER_KHZ) :
